Here's the full performance analysis for `CaseTransformExtensions.cs`. Since you've confirmed `Underscore`/`Pascalize`/`Camelize` are called millions of times, all findings in those methods are elevated to hot-path severity.

---

### Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| `.Substring(` | 2 (L79) |
| `.ToLower()/.ToUpper()` | 2 (L79, L121) |
| `RegexOptions.Compiled` | 8 (L26-28 in `#else`, L36-38 unconditional, L215 per-instance) |
| `GeneratedRegex` | 4 (L13, L16, L19 — only for 3 of 6 regexes) |
| `new Regex(` | 1 (L215 — per-instance in constructor) |
| Chained `.Replace()` | 3 regex Replaces chained on L118-120 |
| `sealed class` | 0 of 1 non-static class |
| `.IndexOf(string)` without `StringComparison` | 0 |
| `params` | 0 |

---

### 🔴 Critical Findings

#### 1. Chained Regex Replace + `.ToLower()` in `Underscore()` — 4 allocations per call
**Impact:** 3 nested `Regex.Replace()` calls each produce an intermediate string, plus `.ToLower()` produces a 4th. At millions of calls, this is millions × 4 avoidable allocations.
**Files:** `CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L115-121`
**Fix:** Replace the triple-regex chain with a single-pass `StringBuilder` or `string.Create()` scan that inserts `_` at casing boundaries and lowercases in one pass. Also convert the 3 `UnderscoreRegex` fields (L36-38) to `[GeneratedRegex]` to match the pattern used for the other regexes.

```csharp
// Optimized single-pass Underscore
public static string Underscore(this string input)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(input)) return input;

    var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length + 4);
    for (int i = 0; i < input.Length; i++)
    {
        char c = input[i];
        if (c == '-' || c == ' ')
        {
            sb.Append('_');
        }
        else if (char.IsUpper(c))
        {
            if (i > 0)
            {
                char prev = input[i - 1];
                // Insert _ between lower→UPPER or between UPPER→UPPER when
                // followed by a lowercase (e.g., "HTML|Parser")
                if (char.IsLower(prev))
                {
                    sb.Append('_');
                }
                else if (char.IsUpper(prev) && i + 1 < input.Length
                         && char.IsLower(input[i + 1]))
                {
                    sb.Append('_');
                }
            }
            sb.Append(char.ToLowerInvariant(c));
        }
        else
        {
            sb.Append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}
```

#### 2. Per-match allocations in `Pascalize()` — 3 allocations per word
**Impact:** `match.Value.Substring(0,1).ToUpper()` + `match.Value.Substring(1)` + string concatenation = 3 allocations per matched word. A 3-word input means 9 intermediate strings per call.
**Files:** `CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L78-79`
**Fix:** Use `char.ToUpperInvariant()` on the first char and avoid `Substring`:

```csharp
return WordPartsRegex().Replace(input, match =>
{
    ReadOnlySpan<char> val = match.ValueSpan;
    return string.Concat(
        char.ToUpperInvariant(val[0]).ToString(),
        val[1..].ToString());
});
